# Git workflow

When asked to review changes and push, always split changes into multiple logical commits grouped by concern instead of one large commit. Each group should be self-contained with a descriptive conventional-commit message (feat:, fix:, chore:, test:, etc.). Example groupings:

- **dependencies** — package.json + lockfile changes only
- **library/core module** — a new .ts file + its tests
- **UI page** — a route page + related component changes (e.g. sidebar nav link)
- **independent features** — separate commits for unrelated additions

After all commits are made, push once. This is the default behavior — no need for the user to ask explicitly.

# GitHub Project workflow

We track planned work in a GitHub Project (V2). All planning goes through `gh`, not custom scripts.

## Project identity

- **Project:** holmgard-lore-editor development
- **Project number:** 3
- **Owner:** FrozenRegister
- **Project node ID:** `PVT_kwHOEMCAyM4BZ3n-`
- **URL:** https://github.com/users/FrozenRegister/projects/3
- **Status field options:** `Todo`, `In Progress`, `Done`

## Auth (one-time per machine)

Classic PATs in the OS keyring need the `project` scope:

```bash
gh auth refresh -s project
gh auth status   # verify "Token scopes" includes "project"
```

Fine-grained PATs need Organization → Projects → Read and Write.

## Common commands

```bash
# List projects for the owner
gh project list --owner FrozenRegister

# List items (returns JSON with id, content.title, content.number, status)
gh project item-list 3 --owner FrozenRegister --format json --limit 50

# Open the project board in the browser
gh project view 3 --owner FrozenRegister --web

# List status field options (one-time, to capture option IDs)
gh project field-list 3 --owner FrozenRegister
```

### Create a new draft item (back-burner work)

```bash
gh project item-create 3 --owner FrozenRegister \
  --title "Phase 1c: wire map tools into the Claude agentic loop" \
  --body "<body markdown>"
```

### Update a draft item body

Get the draft issue content ID first (`gh project item-list` returns the project-item ID `PVTI_*`, but body edits need the draft ID `DI_*` — query via `gh api graphql`):

```bash
gh api graphql -f query='query($iid:ID!){node(id:$iid){... on ProjectV2Item{content{... on DraftIssue{id} ... on Issue{id}}}}}'
# → returns e.g. "DI_xxx" for drafts, or "I_xxx" for real issues

gh project item-edit --id DI_xxx --body "<new body>"
```

### Update a real issue body (preferred for items already promoted to issues)

```bash
gh issue edit <N> --repo FrozenRegister/holmgard-lore-editor --body "<body>"
```

### Update item status

```bash
gh project item-edit \
  --id <PVTI_id> \
  --project-id PVT_kwHOEMCAyM4BZ3n- \
  --field-id <status_field_id> \
  --single-select-option-id <option_id>
```

Run `gh project field-list 3 --owner FrozenRegister --format json` to get the field ID and option IDs.

### Update a custom text/number/date/iteration field

```bash
gh project item-edit --id <PVTI_id> --project-id PVT_kwHOEMCAyM4BZ3n- \
  --field-id <field_id> --text "value"        # or --number / --date / --iteration-id
```

## New-topic convention

**When a new feature, phase, or topic is discussed and is not being implemented immediately, add it to the project as a draft issue.** The body must include everything needed to resume implementation later:

1. **Title** — concise, names the feature (e.g. `Phase 2: Worker endpoints + map tools (holmgard-lore-mcp)`)
2. **Status header** as the first line: `**Status: back-burner. Full spec: <doc-path>**` (or `**Status: active. Implementation prompt:**` if actively in progress)
3. **Implementation prompt** — a copy-pasteable prompt for a future agent session that contains all context, file paths, constraints, and acceptance criteria. If the work lives in a different repo, name that repo in the header and link to its issues/PRs as needed.
4. **Open questions** at the end (if any) — anything the user still needs to decide before implementation.

For topics that cross repos, use separate draft items per repo, each scoped to that repo's slice of the work.

Always confirm with the user before creating items, unless they explicitly say to track the topic.

## Session-end helper: Add-SessionSummary

At the end of a Cline/Claude session, append a draft issue to project #3 summarizing what was done and what the next session should pick up. Use the helper in `scripts/session-end.ps1`:

```powershell
. .\scripts\session-end.ps1
Add-SessionSummary `
  -Title "Short, specific title (no leading `TEST:` outside test runs)" `
  -Summary "1-3 sentences: what landed and what's the state." `
  -Todos "First follow-up", "Second follow-up" `
  -FilesTouched "src/lib/foo.ts", "src/lib/__tests__/foo.test.ts" `
  -Doc "docs/future/<feature>.md" `
  -Status Done       # or "In Progress" or "Todo" (default Todo)
```

The helper builds a body in this standard shape and creates a draft issue via `gh project item-create`:

```markdown
**Status: <Status>. Session: YYYY-MM-DD**

## Summary
<Summary>

## Todos (for next session)
- [ ] <todo 1>
- [ ] <todo 2>

## Files touched
- `path/to/file.ts`
- `path/to/test.ts`

## Context
See [docs/future/<feature>.md](docs/future/<feature>.md) for background and full spec.

Repo: <org>/<repo> | Project: https://github.com/users/FrozenRegister/projects/3
```

When the user asks for a "session summary" or "wrap up" or "log this", call `Add-SessionSummary`. Always pass `-DryRun` first in a non-obvious case to preview the body, then drop `-DryRun` to actually create it. Always confirm with the user before creating the item unless they explicitly asked for the summary.

If `Add-SessionSummary` errors with `missing required scopes [project]`, the gh token needs the **write** project scope. Run `gh auth refresh -h github.com -s project` once and complete the browser prompt.


